label: Normalized Parcel
description: >-
  One county's raw feature, reduced to the five things every county agrees on.
  blue-ribbon's `NormalizedParcelRecord` (apps/etl/src/counties/types.ts).

  ##### THE BEST THING WEIR DID IN THIS EXERCISE #####

  In blue-ribbon, the fact that owner names are dropped here is expressed as
  an ABSENCE: osceola.ts:28 asks the FeatureServer for
  `outFields=PIN,OWNER,PROPCLASS,UNIT,Shape__Area`, and `normalize()` returns
  an object that simply does not mention OWNER. No comment says why. No test
  asserts it. A reviewer adding an owner passthrough breaks no stated rule.
  The other four adapters achieve the same policy by never requesting the
  field -- a different mechanism for the same rule, with nothing reconciling
  them.

  Here, the drop is a declared output schema that the membrane asserts. Adding
  owner to this edge is an edit to a file a human reviews, not an extra key on
  a return object. And because `owner` is declared on the raw side with
  `classification: pii` (see below -- I could not actually do this, see the
  friction), `weir sys` could answer "does pii cross out of the server zone"
  mechanically.

  FRICTION, and it is annoying: I could not label the owner field, because the
  owner field does not exist anywhere in my declarations. It is inside
  RawParcelFeature's opaque JSON blob. So the redaction weir would have made
  visible is invisible for exactly the same reason blue-ribbon's is -- the
  untyped hop swallowed it. The two frictions compound: #1 (no opaque type)
  destroys the payoff of #7 (classification makes redaction checkable). Fixing
  the first is what unlocks the second.
fields:
  pin:
    classification: linkable
    type: utf8
    label: PIN
    description: Canonical dash-separated parcel identification number
    nullable: false
    validations:
      minLength: 8
      maxLength: 40
  county:
    type: utf8
    label: County
    description: The county this parcel is in
    nullable: false
    validations:
      minLength: 4
      maxLength: 20
  townshipFromSource:
    type: utf8
    label: Township From Source
    description: >-
      The township, IF this county's own layer carries one. Osceola derives it
      from its UNIT field; Iosco has no township field at all. Null here is not
      an error -- it is the signal that the statewide MCD lookup must run.
      "A null is data" (start-here.md rule 2).
    nullable: true
    validations:
      maxLength: 100
  acres:
    type: f64
    label: Acres
    description: >-
      Parcel area. Rough passthrough only -- each adapter computes it
      differently (Manistee trusts its own ACRES field; Roscommon divides
      Shape__Area by 4046.8564224 because its Acres field truncates to
      integer), and index.ts overwrites it downstream with a DuckDB-verified
      figure. The spike found disagreeing acreage for one PIN, which is why
      blue-ribbon wraps it in Field<T> at all.
    nullable: false
    validations:
      min: 0
      max: 100000
  boundaryJson:
    # FRICTION: a GeoJSON Polygon is `number[][][]`. weir bans bare arrays at
    # edge boundaries ("an array has no schema-level identity for its
    # elements"). A polygon ring genuinely IS positional -- point 4 follows
    # point 3, and the last repeats the first to close the ring. That is not
    # incidental ordering the graph should be blind to, it is the datum.
    # I cannot declare `many: Coordinate` because coordinates have no key,
    # and inventing one (vertexIndex) is exactly the "order is just data"
    # workaround weir says to avoid. So: another JSON string. See friction #8.
    type: utf8
    label: Boundary JSON
    description: The parcel's polygon boundary, serialized GeoJSON
    nullable: false
    validations:
      minLength: 2
      maxLength: 2000000
  # FRICTION (#14) -- FOUND BY WRITING THE IMPLEMENTATION, NOT BY MODELLING.
  #
  # These four fields are here for no reason this node cares about. The trust
  # value that matters (`sourceTrust`: verified for a direct county fetch,
  # aggregator for one through the fetchgis proxy) is decided two nodes
  # upstream on RawParcelFeature, and is needed two nodes downstream by
  # resolveIdentity's provenance combine. normalizeParcel has no use for it
  # whatsoever and must carry it anyway.
  #
  # I did not notice this while drawing the graph. I noticed it when I wrote
  # resolveIdentity's body and found there was nothing to combine -- the
  # parcel side's provenance had been dropped by an intermediate node that
  # was never asked to preserve it.
  #
  # This is the direct cost of "no ambient state". blue-ribbon does not have
  # this problem, because `Field<T>` attaches provenance to the VALUE at the
  # moment it is created and it rides along inside the payload wherever that
  # value goes. In weir, metadata that must survive a hop has to be a declared
  # field on every intermediate edge, so adding one new piece of cross-cutting
  # metadata is an edit to every edge on the path.
  #
  # It is the classic envelope-vs-payload problem, and weir has an envelope
  # already (correlationId, causationIds, step) -- it is just not extensible.
  # What I want is a declared envelope: metadata that propagates by default
  # along lineage, with a node able to transform it explicitly. That would
  # also be the natural home for a computed classification, which is the
  # feature this whole exercise is for.
  "...Provenanced":
